events.body:
> How is this different from AutoFile? 

`BufferedWriter` flushes through `write_buffer()` (which can throw in the destructor), while `AutoFile::fclose()` returns an error code.

Would you prefer keeping the explicit flushes, adding `Assume(m_buf_pos == 0)`, and catching and logging fallback failures in the destructor, like this:

```cpp
    ~BufferedWriter()
    {
        Assume(m_buf_pos == 0);
        try {
            flush();
        } catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {
            LogError("Failed to flush buffered writer: %s", e.what());
        }
    }
```

